 { Black’s vulnerable kingside The following is an example of when White should play f2-f3 against Black’s ...gxf5 recapture. The reader should take the previous chapter’s games as a reference when studying this game. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Bd3 O-O 6. Nge2 Nc6 7. O-O Nh5 8. Bc2 e5 9. d5 Ne7 10. a4 ( { PH: The computer actually suggests the anti-positional } 10. f4 exf4 11. Nxf4 { here. It turns out that after } Nf6 12. h3 Nd7 13. Be3 Ne5 14. Qe2 { ⩲ Black has a beautiful knight and a nice bishop on g7, but there is very little he can do next, while White has more space and lots of room to further improve his position. } ) 
f5 11. exf5 gxf5 { So far we have closely followed the theory, and now we are in the structure of interest. In contrast to the game Wojtaszek – Fedorov from the previous chapter, White’s bishop is on c2, rather than g2. Hence, playing f2-f3 makes far more sense now. } 12. Bg5 { This occupies a good diagonal in order to obstruct Black’s plans. } ( { If } 12. f4 { the bishop on c2 becomes blocked with } e4 $1 13. Be3 Nf6 14. h3 c5 $5 { when it is clear that White’s kingside or queenside breaks are not easy to carry out. For example, if White insists on a kingside break, then after } 15. Qd2 b6 16. Kh2 Qe8 17. Rg1 Qg6 18. g3 Bd7 19. Nd1 h5 20. Nf2 a6 $5 { ⩱ Black gains queenside counterplay with ...b6-b5 before White accomplishes anything on the kingside. PH: After } c5 { White can take } 15. dxc6 $1 bxc6 16. b4 { , intending b5 to grab space as well as some light squares. White is quite well positioned here. And another nice idea is } 15. a5 $5 Bd7 16. Ba4 { , swapping off White's bad bishop. } ) 
h6 ( { The careless } 12... f4 $2 { hoping for f4-f3, is refuted by } 13. Nd4 $1 { which wins after: } exd4 ( { or } 13... Nf6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 Qc8 16. Nd5 $1 Nexd5 17. cxd5 ) 
14. Qxh5 Bf5 15. Bxe7 Qxe7 16. Bxf5 ) 
13. Bh4 Qe8 14. f3 $1 { An ideal move, clearing the f2-square for the bishop and keeping Black from playing ...e5-e4. } Ng6 15. Bf2 { And now we have a critical position. Black needs to find active play to counter White’s queenside expansion. A possible continuation is: } Qe7 { In the game Sokolov – Stellwagen, Netherlands (ch) } ( { 2004, Black chose } 15... Ngf4 { but after } 16. Nxf4 Nxf4 17. Be3 Qg6 18. Qd2 { the position would have been similar to the main line, and Black’s kingside play is not really getting anywhere. } ) 
16. Kh1 Bd7 17. Qd2 Kh7 18. Be3 Rg8 { Black is hoping for a kingside attack down the g-file, but after: } 19. g4 $1 { It is Black who faces kingside problems since White is winning after: } fxg4 ( { Or: } 19... Nf6 20. gxf5 Nh4 21. Ng3 $18 ) 
20. fxg4 Bxg4 21. Bxg6+ $1 Kxg6 22. Qc2+ { The b1-h7 diagonal proves to be decisive, and the game could finish with: } e4 23. Nxe4 { Threatening Nf6+. } Bf5 24. Rxf5 $1 Kxf5 25. N4g3+ Kg4 26. Qf5+ Kh4 27. Qxh5# { Final remarks 1. What the reader should remember from this example is the combination between White’s bishops on c2 and e3, and the move f2-f3. If either bishop was missing, this set-up would be far less effective. For example, in the game Piorun – Hillarp Persson from the previous chapter, White’s bishop was not on e3, therefore, he could not prevent the knight transfer to f4. 2. White’s g2-g4 break requires precise calculation but is not quite as uncommon as it might seem. It works well together with a bishop on c2. } 
 1-0

