 { Salvijus Bercys – Mauricio Flores Rios, Internet Chess Club 2009 Learning objectives: 1. This game illustrates Black’s plan ...h6-h5 followed by ...Bh6 to activate the dark-squared bishop. 2. Later, this game turns into a King’s Indian Type III-A structure, and this game serves as an example of Black’s ideal play for this pawn formation. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. c4 Bg7 4. Nc3 O-O 5. e4 d6 6. Be2 e5 7. d5 { This is known as the Petrosian system against the King’s Indian. Despite being played on the ICC, this was not a blitz game (we had 90 minutes + 30 seconds increment) . } a5 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bh4 Na6 10. Nd2 Qe8 11. a3 { Preparing b2-b4. } Bd7 ( { The imprecise } 11... Nh7 $6 { allows } 12. b4 $1 { since after } axb4 13. axb4 { the b4-pawn is immune due to the a-file pin. } ) 
12. b3 ( { If } 12. O-O { White must be aware of the option of } a4 $5 { preventing White’s queenside expansion, since } 13. b4 $2 ( { instead } 13. Nb5 $5 { deserves attention } ) 
axb3 14. Nxb3 { is a very undesirable structure transformation for White, because the queenside pawns lose mobility. } ) 
Nh7 ( { PH: The move } 12... Nxe4 { has taken this line out of fashion, the idea being } 13. Ndxe4 f5 14. Nd2 g5 15. Bg4 f4 { , regaining the piece., while after } 14. f3 fxe4 15. Nxe4 { Black can contest the e4-square with } Bf5 $1 16. Bd3 Bxe4 17. Bxe4 Nc5 { , when retreating with } 18. Bc2 { runs into } e4 { . } ) 
13. Rc1 $6 { In this variation the rook does not work so well on the c-file. } ( { The standard line would be } 13. Rb1 h5 14. f3 Bh6 15. O-O Be3 { which is very similar to the game, but with an extra tempo in many lines, as White is ready to play b3-b4 next move. } ) 
h5 14. f3 ( 14. O-O $2 { loses a piece after } g5 15. Bg3 h4 ) 
Bh6 { So far both players have been following standard opening moves; now White has to make an important decision. } 15. O-O ( { The alternative is } 15. Bf2 { but in this case } Qe7 $1 16. O-O h4 { and White’s queenside play is far slower than Black’s attack. For example: } 17. Rc2 Bf4 18. Rb2 Qg5 19. Kh1 Nf6 20. Qe1 Kg7 $1 { Bringing a rook to the h-file, and now: } 21. Bd3 ( 21. Bxh4 Qh6 $1 { followed by ...Rh8 with an attack } ) 
Rh8 22. Ne2 Nh5 $5 23. Bxh4 Qh6 { Black’s attack is very promising. } ) 
Be3+ 16. Bf2 ( { Another option is } 16. Kh1 $5 f5 { though Black is doing well after } 17. Qc2 ( { or } 17. exf5 gxf5 { is similar to the game } ) 
g5 18. Bf2 Bxf2 19. Rxf2 f4 { followed by ...g5-g4 . } ) 
Bxf2+ 17. Rxf2 f5 ( { PH: A good continuation is } 17... h4 { , grabbing a bit of space. } ) 
18. exf5 gxf5 ( { The alternative } 18... Bxf5 { is analyzed separately after this game. } ) 
19. f4 { A standard reply, attempting to fix Black’s centre and attacking the h5-pawn. } ( { Unlike the previous example, if White continues with } 19. Rb1 Qg6 20. b4 axb4 21. axb4 h4 22. Bd3 Nf6 { Black is doing well after ...Nh5-f4 . The key is that White does not have a bishop on e3 to guard the f4-square, and also that Black has a threatening h4-pawn. PH: Actually, White is doing very well in this line after } 22. h3 $5 { when } Nf6 $2 { is answered by } 23. f4 $1 { when suddenly ...Nh5 becomes impossible. } ) 
Nf6 20. Nf1 ( { Black is doing well after } 20. fxe5 dxe5 { , though the resulting structure is less relevant to our study. } ) 
Qg6 ( { Defining the structure immediately with } 20... e4 $5 { has the drawback of leaving the knight on a6 out of the game. For example, } 21. Rb1 Qg6 22. b4 Kh8 23. Ne3 Rg8 24. Qd4 $14 { and White has a nice and solid position. } ) 
21. h3 $6 { Preventing ...Ng4, but seriously weakening the g3-square. } ( { Better was } 21. fxe5 dxe5 22. d6 $5 c6 { with a dynamic balance. } ) 
Nc5 $1 { This move is natural and strong, bringing the knight into the game and heading towards the weak g3-square. } 22. Rf3 ( { Now } 22. fxe5 dxe5 $17 { doesn’t help since Black has many potential threats such as ...Kh8 and ...Rg8 followed by ...Nce4 , or ...f5-f4 followed by ...Bxh3 . } ) 
Kh8 { An ideal opportunity to create threats down the g-file. } 23. b4 ( { If } 23. Rg3 Qh6 24. fxe5 h4 $1 25. Re3 f4 26. Rf3 dxe5 $17 { and Black has great attacking prospects. } ) 
axb4 24. axb4 Nce4 25. Nxe4 Nxe4 ( { Somewhat better was } 25... fxe4 $5 26. Rg3 Qf5 27. fxe5 h4 $1 28. Rgc3 Qxe5 $15 { when Black has good chances for a kingside attack. } ) 
26. Bd3 $2 { Making things easier for Black. } ( { Much better was } 26. Qc2 $1 { preventing } Ra2 { , though after } exf4 27. Rxf4 Qh6 28. Rf3 Qg7 $1 { ⩱ Black has a promising kingside attack. } ) 
Ra2 $1 27. Bc2 ( { White loses material after } 27. Rc2 $2 Ba4 $1 28. Qb1 Nc3 $1 { – the key move. } ) 
Rg8 28. Ne3 ( { White is already losing after: } 28. Qe2 $2 exf4 $1 { Threatening 29...Ng5 , and } 29. Rxf4 { is met by } Qg5 $1 { pinning the rook! } 30. Qf3 Ra3 $1 31. Ne3 Rxe3 $19 ) 
Ra3 { Threatening 29...Rxe3 because of the mate on g2. } 29. Qe2 Ng3 30. Qd2 e4 { Finally it is a good moment to define the central chain, since Black’s knight is now on the superb g3-square, instead of the poor a6-square. } ( { Even stronger though is: } 30... exf4 $1 31. Rxf4 Qg5 32. Qd4 Rg7 33. Re1 Ne4 $1 { (threatening ...Qxf4 and ...Rxe3) } 34. Rxe4 fxe4 35. Bxe4 Bxh3 $19 ) 
31. Rf2 Qf6 32. Bb1 Rga8 33. Qe1 h4 { Reinforcing control of the g3-square. } 34. Rd2 Rb3 35. b5 b6 $1 { ∓ And we have a dream position for Black in this transformed King’s Indian Type III-A, for the following reasons: 1. Black has full control of the a-file. 2. Black has a strong knight on g3, ideally supported by the h4-pawn. 3. White’s f4-pawn is practically isolated and vulnerable. 4. White does not have any reactions on the queenside. } 36. Nc2 { Unfortunately, at this point time trouble began and I spoiled my position. The game however, could have finished very nicely with the variation: } Rc3 $5 ( { Or } 36... e3 $1 37. Nxe3 Re8 38. Rd3 Rxd3 39. Bxd3 Qd4 { winning a piece. } ) 
 ( { Instead I played } 36... Ra4 $6 { and later blundered and made a draw. } ) 
37. Ne3 Raa3 38. Rxc3 Qxc3 39. Kf2 Be8 $1 { -+ Heading to h5, bringing the last piece into the game, while White is paralyzed. } 40. Rd1 Qb2+ 41. Rd2 Qb3 42. Rd1 Bh5 43. Rd2 Ra1 44. Bc2 Qxe3+ { A nice final blow. } 45. Qxe3 ( { Or } 45. Kxe3 Rxe1 { wins simply. } ) 
Nh1# { It’s a shame I missed the opportunity to finish off the game with a fun variation like this one. Final remarks 1. White’s main problem in this position was losing control of the g3-square. As we may recall from the game Wojtaszek – Fedorov from the previous chapter, White’s kingside break g3-g4 was very strong, and not having such a plan in this position is already disappointing. 2. Black’s key move was 21...Nc5, bringing the knight into the game, heading towards g3, before closing the central structure with ...e5-e4. 3. White’s imprecise play allowed Black to gain crucial tempos on moves 26-30, helping Black achieve his dream position. 4. Once Black installed his knight on g3 and doubled rooks on the a-file, there was essentially nothing White could hope for, and the sample variation I chose to finish off this game illustrates that. } 
 0-1

