 { White’s knight controls e4 If Black had taken 18...Bxf5 instead of 18...gxf5 in the game Bercys – Flores Rios, we would have reached a King’s Indian Type III-B structure in which White easily controls the e4-square after: } 19. Nde4 $14 { Consequently White stands somewhat better. To see why this is the case, I offer the following variation: } Qe7 ( { If } 19... h4 { the direct } 20. c5 $5 Nxc5 21. Nxc5 dxc5 22. Bc4 Kg7 23. Qe1 $1 { ⩲ secures an advantage for White as the e5-pawn will fall after Re2xe5. } ) 
20. Bd3 { Reinforcing e4. } ( { PH: Here } 20. c5 $5 { is an interesting pawn sacrifice, when after } Nxc5 21. Nxc5 dxc5 22. Bc4 Kh8 23. Re2 $14 { White has considerable pressure. With that in mind, on the previous move, } b6 $5 { would have been a good idea for Black. } ) 
Nf6 ( { Worse is } 20... Rf7 21. Qd2 Raf8 22. Rb1 g5 23. b4 axb4 24. axb4 { when Black’s position is near collapse due to the threat of c4-c5. For example: } Bxe4 ( { Or } 24... g4 25. fxg4 hxg4 26. Qh6 $1 { +- } ) 
25. Nxe4 g4 26. Qh6 $1 gxf3 27. Qxh5 fxg2 28. Rxg2 { When it is White who has a winning kingside attack. } ) 
21. Rb1 Nxe4 22. Nxe4 Kg7 23. b4 axb4 24. axb4 $14 { Black’s knight is out of play and White will soon carry out the desired c4-c5 break, while Black is unable to create serious kingside threats. Let’s see what happens to an optimistic approach: } g5 $6 { Such play is more likely to backfire than it is to succeed. } ( { PH: A sound positional way of continuing is } 24... Nb8 $1 { intending ...Nd7 next, covering c5, while the rook can become active via the a-file. } ) 
25. Qe2 Bg6 ( { Or } 25... Rf7 $2 26. Ng3 $1 Bxd3 27. Qxd3 { winning material due to the threats Nxh5 and Nf5. } ) 
26. Qe3 Kh8 27. Ra2 $16 { Black’s g5- and h5-pawns are more liabilities than strengths. White’s queenside play will proceed with c4-c5 gaining a big advantage. } 
