 { Olivier Renet – Anish Giri, Mulhouse 2011 Learning objective: By contrast with the previous game fragment, this game teaches us how Black may create kingside counterplay by fighting for the e4-square, expanding on the kingside, and eventually achieving the ...g5-g4 break. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Be2 O-O 6. Nf3 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. Bd2 ( { PH: I think it's better to refrain from playing } 11. f3 { too early, as then Black can reply } f4 { and he has gotten White to play f2-f3 without having had to move the d7-knight to f6, so this knight can remain useful in restraining White's initiative on the queenside. } ) 
fxe4 ( { A more popular alternative is } 11... Nf6 { which is examined in the last game of this chapter. } ) 
12. Nxe4 Nf5 { We have reached the position of interest and so far both players have followed theory closely, but now White deviates with: } 13. Rc1 { This supports c4-c5 in order to gain queenside play. The position is typical, though not very deeply explored by theory, and it is a good moment to assess the situation. As we know, White desires to obtain firm control of the e4-square, and he will accomplish this after 14.f3 and 15.Ndf2. Hence, it makes sense for Black to begin fighting for the e4-square immediately. } ( { White was more likely to secure a tiny advantage after } 13. f3 Nf6 14. Ndf2 Nd4 15. Bd3 { with good control of the e4-square. For example, } Nh5 16. Re1 Nf4 17. Bf1 $14 { when White’s kingside remains solid and he may proceed with 18.Bc3 to expel the knight from d4 . } ) 
Nf6 $1 { Getting ahead of White’s consolidating moves f2-f3 and Ndf2. } ( { An alternative was } 13... b6 $5 { preventing c4-c5, and if White insists with } 14. b4 $6 { then } Nf6 15. Bg5 a5 $5 16. a3 axb4 17. axb4 Nd4 $15 { when it is pretty clear that White’s c4-c5 break will not yield much. } ) 
14. Bg5 ( { If } 14. Nxf6 Qxf6 { the threat is ...e5-e4 , and after } 15. Bf3 Nd4 16. Be4 Bf5 $1 { (again fighting for e4) } 17. Re1 c5 $5 { Black has equalized. He may follow up with ...g6-g5-g4 and possibly ...Bh6 afterwards, trading his ‘bad’ bishop. } ) 
 ( { Also } 14. f3 Nxe4 15. fxe4 Nd4 { yields an approximately level position after: } 16. Rxf8 Bxf8 17. Be3 Qh4 18. Nf2 Bd7 $5 ) 
h6 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 { White retains his control of e4 at the cost of giving up his dark-squared bishop, hence becoming vulnerable on the dark squares. } 16. Bg4 b6 { Preventing c4-c5. } ( { White was probably hoping for } 16... Nd4 17. Bxc8 Qxc8 { trading Black’s valuable light-squared bishop, and now } 18. c5 $14 { secures a small advantage for White due to the creation of a weakness on d6 . } ) 
17. Rc3 $6 { This is not exactly a mistake, but it is certainly the beginning of an unnatural plan. } ( { The alternative was } 17. Qd2 a5 18. f3 Bg7 { when Black has good counterplay associated with ...h6-h5 and ...Bh6 , but White may attempt a slow expansion with } 19. b3 $5 { following up with a2-a3 and b3-b4 } ( { or } 19. Ndf2 h5 20. Bxf5 Bxf5 21. Ng5 Bh6 22. h4 { with level chances } ) 
 ) 
 ( { If White insists on c4-c5 with } 17. b4 { then } a5 $1 18. a3 axb4 19. axb4 { and Black has a good position after } Bg5 $5 20. Ra1 Rxa1 21. Qxa1 h5 22. Bxf5 Bxf5 { . } ) 
a5 { Preventing b2-b4; it will be difficult and slow for White to create queenside play after this. } 18. Ne1 Bg7 19. Nc2 h5 { This is a standard move, expanding on the kingside and clearing the h6-square for the bishop. } 20. Bh3 ( { If } 20. Bxf5 $6 gxf5 { Black gains good control of key central squares: } 21. Ng3 ( { or } 21. Nd2 h4 $15 ) 
h4 22. Nh5 f4 $5 23. Nxg7 Kxg7 $15 { Black has good attacking prospects on the kingside. } ) 
 ( { Also } 20. Be2 Qh4 21. Bd3 Bd7 { , followed by doubling rooks on the f-file, gives Black a pleasant position. } ) 
Bh6 { Now Black’s bad bishop moves onto a very good diagonal, and his position is at least equal. } 21. Re1 Kh7 { Black progresses slowly, while White is running out of ideas and natural plans to implement. } 22. Ng3 ( { Attempting a queenside expansion with } 22. b3 Bd7 23. a3 { does not really help since after } Qe7 { planning to double rooks on the f-file, White cannot play } 24. b4 $6 { due to the unpleasant: } axb4 25. axb4 Ba4 26. Qe2 Bxc2 27. Qxc2 Nd4 $15 ) 
Qg5 23. Rf3 Bd7 24. b3 Rf7 25. Re4 $6 { This rook has nothing to do on e4. } ( { More logical was } 25. a3 Raf8 26. Ne4 Qe7 27. Rd3 { keeping an eye on the h5-pawn, preventing ...g6-g5; now } b5 $5 { gives Black good fighting chances on both flanks. } ) 
Raf8 $15 { Black has doubled rooks on the f-file, while White’s artificial piece arrangement does little for his position. } 26. Re1 Qe7 ( { An interesting alternative was } 26... Qd2 $5 27. Qxd2 Bxd2 28. Rd1 Bh6 { since White’s pieces are poorly arranged on h3 , g3 and f3 , and in fact, there are no natural moves for White. For example: } 29. Ne4 $2 ( 28... Qe7 28. Rf1 { allows } b5 $5 { ⩱ creating a new front of attack } ) 
 ( { and } 28... Qe7 28. Nxf5 $6 gxf5 $15 { is just unpleasant } ) 
 { Black now wins with } g5 30. Bxf5 Bxf5 31. Re1 g4 32. Rd3 Bxe4 33. Rxe4 Rxf2 ) 
27. Ne4 Bf4 { Preparing ...g6-g5. } 28. Rd3 Bh6 29. Rf3 Qe8 { Protecting the h5-pawn, preparing ...g6-g5. } ( { The optimistic } 29... g5 $2 { is met by } 30. Rxf5 $1 Bxf5 31. Bxf5 Rxf5 32. Qxh5 { when Black is an exchange up for a pawn, but White has undisputed control of the e4-square and the light squares, giving him reasonable counterplay. } ) 
 ( { Of course Black is not interested in repeating moves with: } 29... Bf4 $6 ) 
30. a4 $2 { This move is bad for two reasons: 1. It permanently removes the possibility of a2-a3, b3-b4 and c4-c5, which is White’s only plan on the queenside. 2. It allows Black’s strong reply 30...g5. } ( { It was necessary to prevent } . a4 g6-g5 { for the moment with } 30. Rd3 { , but after } Rg7 { the threat } g6-g5-g4 { gives White some difficulties. A possible continuation is } 31. Qe2 { g5 32.g3 } g4 { 33.Bg2 h4⩱ when Black is calling the shots on the kingside, while White’s queenside play is still on standby. } ) 
g5 $1 { ∓ Threatening ...g5-g4, forcing White to give away his light-squared bishop. } 31. Bxf5+ Bxf5 { Now Black is ready to embark on a kingside expansion, and even dispute the control of the e4-square. White’s position is rather aimless. } 32. Ng3 Bg6 33. Rxf7+ ( { Heading towards e6 with } 33. Nd4 $2 { fails to the simple: } g4 $1 34. Rxf7 Qxf7 35. Ne6 Qxf2 $19 ) 
Qxf7 34. f3 $2 { Making things much easier for Black. } ( { A better defence was } 34. Qe2 { though after } g4 $5 35. Ne3 Qf4 36. Rd1 Kg7 37. Ngf1 Bg5 { Black has a big advantage due to the many potential threats, such as ...h5-h4-h3 , or ...h5-h4 followed by ...g4-g3 . } ) 
g4 $1 { -+ The beginning of the end; note how all of Black’s pieces contribute to the attack. } 35. Rf1 { Other moves lose on the spot. } ( { For example, } 35. fxg4 $2 { loses a piece to } Qf2 36. Kh1 Bxc2 ) 
Kh8 $5 { A nice move, clearing the h7-square for the queen, and preparing to meet 36.fxg4 with 36...Bxc2. } ( 35... gxf3 36. Rxf3 Bf4 $17 { followed by ...h5-h4 was good too. } ) 
36. Qe2 ( { The try } 36. fxg4 $2 { is met by } Bxc2 $1 37. Qxc2 ( { or } 37. Rxf7 Bxd1 $19 ) 
Be3 38. Kh1 Qxf1 39. Nxf1 Rxf1 ) 
 ( { If } 36. Na3 h4 37. fxg4 ( { or } 37. Ne2 Be3 38. Kh1 h3 $1 { -+ } ) 
Be3 $1 38. Kh1 hxg3 $1 39. Rxf7 Rxf7 { when Black wins nicely after: } 40. Qe2 ( 40. hxg3 $2 Rh7 ) 
Bc5 41. Nb5 Rf2 42. Qe1 Be4 ) 
Qh7 37. Ne1 h4 38. Nh1 g3 $1 { If White does not allow the kingside to be opened, his knight will be trapped on h1 forever. } 39. hxg3 ( { If } 39. h3 e4 { Black is basically a piece up, whereas opening the position decides the game on the spot. } ) 
hxg3 40. Nxg3 Bf4 { White is helpless and the rest is rather simple. } 41. Nh1 ( { If } 41. Kf2 { then } Rg8 { threatening ...Bxg3 followed by ...Bd3 , and now } 42. Nh1 e4 { with a winning attack. } ) 
Qh2+ 42. Kf2 e4 43. Rg1 ( 43. fxe4 $2 { loses immediately: } Qh4 44. g3 Bxg3 45. Ke3 ( { or } 45. Kg1 Rxf1 46. Qxf1 Qh2 ) 
Qxe4 46. Kd2 Bxe1 47. Kxe1 Qb1 ) 
Re8 44. Kf1 exf3 45. Qb2+ Kh7 ( { White resigns. The f3-pawn cannot be taken since } 45... Kh7 45. Nxf3 { loses to } Bd3 47. Kf2 Be3 { Final remarks 1. One of Black’s most important moves was 13...Nf6 forcing White to choose between giving up his e4-square or giving up his dark squares, as he did in the game. 2. Later, the control of the e4-square played a significant role. As the reader may have noticed, White’s position began to fall apart once his knight was forced out of e4, on move 32. 3. As we could observe in this game, Black should have good fighting chances along the f-file as long as he can control the c4-c5 break with pawns on a5 and b6, as he did in the game. } ) 

