 { Piece Arrangement – after Black plays ...f5-f4 Previously we have studied what happens when White responds to ...f7-f5 by capturing exf5. Assuming White does not do so, then Black will probably push ...f5-f4, yielding the position we are about to study now. I would like to discuss how Black should arrange his pieces in one of the most typical situations where this structure will arise. The position in this diagram has arisen in nearly 3000 database games, and although it is clear White will play on the queenside and Black will play for a mating attack, it remains an open question how exactly these plans should be carried out. Let’s consider the relatively classical approach: } 13. Nd3 { Preparing the c4-c5 break. } ( { Nowadays many players prefer } 13. Rc1 Ng6 14. c5 $5 Nxc5 15. b4 Na6 16. Nb5 { with a complex position you will find in many opening books. } ) 
 ( { Or } 13. g4 $5 { giving rise to a type of position we will examine in the last two examples. } ) 
 ( { PH: In my youth there was development of the line } 13. Nb5 $5 { , when } b6 { and only then ...a6 is probably necessary, as after the immediate } a6 14. Na7 $1 { White is set to grab the c8-bishop, making Black's attacking prospects on the kingside rather slim. } Rxa7 15. Bxa7 b6 { is critical, as Black is threatening to trap the bishop, but after } 16. b4 Bb7 17. Rc1 { White is threatening to crash through, for instance } Nc8 18. c5 dxc5 19. bxc5 Nxc5 20. Rxc5 $1 { and the bishop will get out next. } ) 
 ( { PH: My personal favorite line was } 13. a4 { , intending } h5 14. Nb5 b6 15. a5 { , and if } a6 16. axb6 { Black can't take the knight. } ) 
 { And here is the piece arrangement I would like to discuss. Today’s typical continuation for Black would be: } Nf6 ( { A far less popular plan is } 13... Rf6 $5 { to follow with ...Rh6 and ...Qe8-h5 . } ) 
14. c5 Ng6 15. Rc1 Rf7 { This is a key move! It will be followed by ...Bf8 and ...Rg7. This idea was introduced in the early 1950’s and is still used today. The point is to cover the c7-square from White’s invasion cxd6 followed by Nb5-c7. At the same time, Black’s bishop protects the d6-pawn and the rook on g7 will support a kingside attack. } 16. Rc2 Bf8 ( { PH: } 16... h5 { is today's computer's move. } ) 
17. cxd6 ( { PH: The computer comes up with a sophisticated plan here: } 17. c6 b6 { and now } 18. g4 h5 19. h3 { - thanks to the inclusion of c6 ...b6, Black's queenside bishop and rook are both completely passive. } ) 
cxd6 18. Qd2 g4 19. Rfc1 g3 $5 { We see a pawn sacrifice that is typical and frequently used by King’s Indian players. The point of this sacrifice is to open the f- and g-files, create kingside activity, and distract White’s forces from their already-advanced queenside play. This sacrifice was first introduced in the game Taimanov – Najdorf from the Candidates of 1953. That game continued: } 20. hxg3 fxg3 21. Bxg3 Nh5 22. Bh2 Be7 { Black had excellent compensation for the material, and went on to win the game very convincingly. The rest of this game can be found in the database, or in many classical King’s Indian books. } 
