 { Mads Andersen – Krisztian Szabo, Budapest 2013 Learning objective: In this game we see the power of Black’s kingside attack based on the ...g6-g5-g4-g3 plan. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 { This is the beginning of one of the most analyzed (and most interesting) openings in the history of chess. It is known as the Mar del Plata variation, and has a history that dates back to the 1940’s. Among its early developers were Najdorf, Gligoric, Petrosian and many others. Many of their ideas are still used in today’s games. I will go through the opening phase without making too many comments for a simple reason: there exist very extensive treatments of this variation already, and any amount of comment here would be superficial. } 9. Ne1 Ne8 { The knight moves to e8 to cover the c7-square against a future threat consisting of Rc1, cxd6 and Nb5-c7. } ( { An alternative is } 9... Nd7 { and after } 10. Be3 f5 11. f3 f4 12. Bf2 g5 13. Nd3 { we reach the position mentioned before this game, where we discussed one of Black’s ideal piece set-ups. } ) 
10. Be3 f5 11. f3 f4 12. Bf2 h5 13. c5 g5 14. Rc1 ( { A far more common alternative is: } 14. a4 $5 { PH: This is also the computer's preferred move. If play continues as in the game - } Ng6 15. a5 Rf7 { , the idea is } 16. c6 $5 bxc6 17. dxc6 Be6 18. Ba6 g4 19. Bb7 { - this is messy, but if White doesn't get mated he is crashing through on the queenside. } ) 
Ng6 15. cxd6 cxd6 16. Nb5 { At this point we are essentially outside the opening book, and we can begin the analysis of the game. } ( { Previously } 16. a4 Bh6 17. Nd3 Bd7 18. Nb5 a6 19. Na3 { had been played in the game Mchedlishvili – Rapport, Plovdiv 2012, but after } g4 { Black has good counterplay. } ) 
a6 ( { In some similar positions, Black just plays } 16... Rf7 { and after } 17. Nxa7 Bd7 { the kingside attack keeps on going, but this is not one of those cases, as after } 18. a4 Bh6 19. Bb5 $1 { White can secure a stable advantage: } Rg7 20. Bxd7 Qxd7 21. Nb5 g4 22. Kh1 { As we will see in the next game, the attack loses most of its energy once light-squared bishops are off the board. For example, } g3 23. Bb6 gxh2 24. Kxh2 $16 { with a healthy extra pawn. PH: And if } h4 { then } 24. h3 $1 { and the lack of a light-squared bishop really hurts Black. } ) 
17. Na3 { Threatening Nc4-b6. } b5 { Covering the key c4-square. } 18. Nac2 $1 { Now that the c4-square is covered, the c6-square is weakened, so the knight heads in this direction. } Nf6 19. Nb4 g4 20. Nc6 ( { If } 20. Qc2 { , threatening to trade queens with } Qc7 { , then } g3 $1 21. hxg3 fxg3 22. Bxg3 h4 23. Bf2 ( { or } 23. Nc6 Qd7 24. Bf2 Bh6 $13 { followed by ...Qg7 } ) 
Nh5 24. Ned3 Qg5 $15 { with more than enough compensation, in the spirit of the game Taimanov – Najdorf, Zurich 1953, as mentioned earlier. } ) 
Qe8 { We have reached the critical moment in the game, and the utmost precision is required from both sides. I should emphasize that White’s five-move knight manoeuvre to c6 is actually far more common than it might seem; the knight is quite strong on c6, and disrupts Black’s coordination. On the other hand, White’s queenside attack is not as powerful anymore, since the only aggressive plan lies in a2-a4. In contrast, Black is ready to sacrifice a pawn next move with ...g4-g3. Should White worry? We will see in a moment. For now, I would say we have a relatively standard Mar del Plata position, which in this case offers objectively better chances to White, but just one slip is enough for Black to seize the opportunity to execute a near-decisive kingside attack. } 21. Kh1 $2 { This is where everything goes wrong for White. Although this is not exactly a mistake, it implies that White will respond to 21...g3 with 22.Bg1, which is just a bad plan as we will see. } ( { The right approach was: } 21. a4 $1 { This threatens the queenside before it is too late. } g3 ( { Or } 21. Kh1 h4 22. fxg4 Nxe4 23. Bb6 $14 { when Black has fighting chances in an inferior position. } ) 
22. Bb6 $1 { Keeping the kingside as closed as possible. } ( { If } 22. hxg3 fxg3 23. Bxg3 Bh6 { Black gains the usual compensation for the pawn. } ) 
bxa4 ( { PH: } 22. Kh1 Nh7 $1 { is the computer's move, the idea being to get the queen to h4 eventually. For instance } 23. axb5 axb5 24. Bxb5 Qf7 25. Qb3 { . Now if } Qf6 { White can defend with } 26. Kh1 Qh4 27. Bg1 { , so instead } h4 26. h3 Ng5 { Black is threatening to sac on h3, so } 27. Nd8 Qf6 28. Ne6 Nxe6 29. dxe6 Bxe6 30. Bc4 Bxc4 31. Qxc4 Qf7 { and Black is a bit better in the ending. } ) 
23. Qxa4 gxh2 24. Kxh2 h4 25. Qa3 $1 { Attacking the d6-pawn and disrupting Black’s piece coordination. } Qd7 26. Na5 Ne8 27. Rh1 { And if: } h3 $6 28. Kg1 $16 { It seems like Black will experience more kingside problems than White will. } ) 
g3 22. Bg1 $6 ( { Accepting the sacrifice with } 22. hxg3 fxg3 23. Bxg3 { would be inconsistent with 21.Kh1 , but objectively better as after: } h4 24. Bf2 Nh5 25. f4 $1 { The key move. } Nhxf4 ( { or } 25. Bg1 Ngxf4 $5 26. Bxh4 Bf6 { with a complex game } ) 
26. Bg4 $14 { Black’s kingside attack is unlikely to succeed. PH: Instead of 24...Nh5 Black should play } Bh6 $1 { to counter the intended f4. } ) 
gxh2 23. Bf2 $2 { White has been heading towards this position for the last three moves, and here we are. Is it a good position for White? Throughout the last decade I have heard innumerable respectable players explain to me, or others, how every time that Black plays the sacrifice ...g4-g3, White should simply react with Bg1 and after ...gxh2 then Bf2! would terminate Black’s attack, because the h2-pawn would serve as a shield. I presume this piece of wisdom is mostly based on hearsay, so here I would like to put in writing how wrong, or at least misleading, this advice is. Black’s attack simply moves on, and as the reader will see, there is no reason to believe White’s position is safer because of the h2-pawn. } ( { Better was } 23. Bxh2 { , though after } h4 24. a4 Nh5 25. Kg1 Ng3 26. Rf2 h3 $1 { it is Black who plays for the advantage. For example, } 27. axb5 axb5 28. Bxb5 hxg2 29. Nxg2 Bh3 { when Black’s kingside attack is far more promising than White’s extra pawn. } ) 
h4 $1 { ⩱ Preparing ...Nh5-g3. Maybe this is too early, but I would dare to say that White’s position is near desperate already, as Black has taken the initiative and White no longer finds the time to carry out any aggressive queenside plans. In fact, after several hours of analysis, I could not find any decent continuation for White. For now I just call this position ”slight advantage for Black” though I do not think White can prevent the position from becoming ”big advantage for Black”! } 24. Nd3 ( { The attempt } 24. a4 bxa4 25. Qxa4 { is too slow, and can be met by } Nh5 $5 26. Kxh2 Ng3 $17 { with a huge advantage due to the potential threat ...h4-h3 . } ) 
Nh5 25. Kxh2 { Thus White is forced to capture his ”shield” h2-pawn after all. It becomes clear that White’s manoeuvre Bg1-f2 was a mere waste of moves. } ( { It is too late to start with } 25. a4 Ng3 26. Kxh2 ( { or } 26. Bxg3 fxg3 $19 { followed by ...h4-h3 } ) 
h3 $1 ( { or } 26. Kxh2 bxa4 $5 ) 
27. Rg1 Nh4 $17 { and Black’s attack is very powerful. } ) 
Ng3 26. Rg1 Rf7 { Preparing to transfer to the h-file. } ( { A tempting alternative is } 26... Qd7 $5 { threatening ...h4-h3xg2 followed by ...Qh3 , though after } 27. Be1 $1 h3 28. Bxg3 fxg3 29. Kxg3 Bh6 $15 { White’s king seems to be safer than in the game. } ) 
27. Bb6 ( { If } 27. Be1 Bf6 28. Nf2 { White gets an unpleasant position that is similar to the game after: } h3 $1 29. gxh3 Rh7 $17 { When a possible continuation is } 30. Qd3 Qf8 31. Bf1 Qh6 32. Ng4 Bxg4 33. fxg4 Bh4 { when the threat of ...Nxf1 and ...Bg3 wins in basically every variation, such as: } 34. Rc3 Rf8 35. Qf3 Nxf1 36. Rxf1 Bg3 $1 { -+ } ) 
Bf6 28. Nf2 h3 $1 { ∓ A great sacrifice. By giving up this pawn Black obtains an open g-file and a semi-open h-file to bring White’s king under fire. There are so many threats that White cannot take advantage of his extra pawn, let alone be able to make further progress on the queenside. } ( { Note that } 28... Rh7 $5 { also gives excellent attacking chances to Black. A long but very instructive variation is: } 29. Nh3 { (it is necessary to block ...h4-h3) } Nf8 30. a4 { Searching for counterplay, but Black just keeps on going: } Rg7 31. axb5 axb5 32. Bd3 ( { or } 31... h3 31. Bxb5 Bxh3 33. gxh3 Rb7 { winning a piece } ) 
Nh7 33. Rc2 Ng5 $1 { -+ Lifting the h3 blockade. White can only wait and see how Black breaks through the defence with a well-prepared ...h4-h3: } 34. Nxg5 Rxg5 35. Bf1 Qh5 36. Qe1 Kg7 37. Rf2 h3 38. gxh3 Bxh3 $1 { With forced mate. } ) 
29. gxh3 Rb7 30. Ba5 Rh7 31. Bf1 Bh4 { Bringing the bishop into the attack; a threat is ...Nxf1 and ...Bg3, nearly winning. } ( { Another strong plan is } 31... Nh8 $5 { to follow with ...Nf7-g5 attacking the h3-pawn, and now after } 32. Nh1 Qd7 $1 33. Nf2 ( 32... Bh4 32. Nxg3 $2 Rxh3 $1 { -+ } ) 
Qg7 34. Nh1 Bh4 $17 { White is in big trouble. } ) 
32. Bg2 { This move accelerates the loss, though I believe there was no way to survive against precise play from Black. } ( { More stubborn was } 32. Bd3 Bg5 33. Bf1 Qf8 34. Rc2 Qh6 $17 { though the attack is easy to carry out and White’s position seems rather desperate. } ) 
Nf5 $1 { -+ This rather surprising blow decides the game very nicely and quickly. White’s king is so vulnerable that even an extra piece will not help him survive. } 33. exf5 ( { Declining the piece sacrifice with } 33. Bb6 { does not help as after } Bg3 34. Kh1 Ne3 35. Bxe3 fxe3 { White is just lost. For example, } 36. Ng4 Bxg4 37. fxg4 Bf2 38. Rf1 Qf8 { when there is no defence against the threat of ...Qf4-g3 and ...Rxh3 since } 39. Qf3 $2 { loses to: } Qxf3 40. Bxf3 Rxh3 41. Kg2 Nf4 ) 
 ( { Neither does } 33. Ng4 { help: } Bg3 34. Kh1 Ne3 35. Qe2 Bxg4 36. fxg4 Qd7 { With decisive threats such as ...Nxg4 or ...Kg7 followed by ...Rah8xh3 . } ) 
Bxf2 34. fxg6 ( { Or } 34. Rf1 Bg3 35. Kg1 Nh4 { and wins. } ) 
Qxg6 { There is nothing to do against the checkmating threat ...Qg3 and ...Bxh3; note the minor role White’s knight has played in the fight. } 35. Qf1 ( { Even easier is: } 35. Rf1 Qg3 36. Kh1 Rxh3 37. Bxh3 Qxh3 ) 
Qg3+ 36. Kh1 Kf8 { The most precise, getting out of the g-file pin. } 37. Nxe5 Bxh3 38. Ng6+ Ke8 { White resigns as mate is unavoidable. Final remarks 1. The biggest fact to remember from this game is the ineffectiveness of White’s moves 21-23 – that is, the plan of Kh1 and then Bf2-g1-f2. Although this is a typical response to Black’s ...g6-g5-g4-g3 plan, it does not work as well as many believe. 2. Black’s 23...h4! played a key role in this win, as it is the best way to open lines on the kingside in order to checkmate White’s king. The plan ...Nh5-g3 followed by ...h4-h3 is very strong and practically irresistible. } 
 0-1

