 { Sergei Azarov – Karsten Volke, Dresden 2007 Learning objective: This game illustrates how difficult it is for Black to create serious kingside threats without his light-squared bishop. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 d6 5. O-O Bd7 6. Re1 g6 7. d4 { There is a nice concept behind this theoretical move. White will follow with d4-d5 obtaining the structure studied in this chapter under favourable circumstances. The reason is that White will be able to trade his bad bishop for Black’s good bishop. As we will see in this game, this trade greatly reduces Black’s chances for a mating attack. } Bg7 8. d5 Ne7 9. Bxd7+ Nxd7 10. Be3 O-O ( { Another option was } 10... h6 { (preparing ...f7-f5) } 11. Nfd2 f5 12. f3 { and now } Bf6 13. Bf2 h5 { gave Black perfectly reasonable counterplay in the game Felgaer – Sokolov, Istanbul (ol) (2012) } ( { or } 13... Bg5 $5 ) 
 ) 
11. c4 ( { Of course not } 11. Nfd2 $2 f5 12. f3 f4 13. Bf2 g5 14. c4 h5 { when Black has an extra tempo compared to the game. } ) 
h6 { Covering the g5-square, preparing ...f7-f5. } ( { The immediate } 11... f5 $6 { does not work as well due to } 12. Ng5 { threatening Ne6 , and after } f4 13. Bc1 ( { but not } 12... h6 12. Ne6 $2 fxe3 $1 ) 
Nc5 14. b4 Bf6 15. Nxh7 Kxh7 16. bxc5 dxc5 17. Nd2 $16 { White is much better after Nf3 and Bb2 , pressuring the e5-pawn. } ) 
12. Nfd2 f5 13. f3 $14 { White has achieved his ideal piece formation with Be3, Nd2 and a knight coming to c3, and he stands a little better. } f4 14. Bf2 g5 15. Nc3 ( { The premature } 15. b4 $2 { is met by } a5 $1 { forcing White to spoil his structure, since the usual } 16. a3 $2 { loses a pawn to } axb4 { due to the a-file pin. PH: Structure doesn't matter that much here. After 15...a5 White can just play } 16. bxa5 Rxa5 17. a4 b6 18. Nb3 Ra8 19. Nc3 { , intending a5 next, with a clear edge. } ) 
Ng6 ( { Another option was } 15... a5 16. a3 Ng6 17. b4 b6 { and now White needs a quick method for opening up the queenside before Black gains kingside counterplay. A possible continuation is } 18. Na4 $5 axb4 19. axb4 h5 20. c5 bxc5 21. bxc5 Qe7 22. cxd6 cxd6 23. Nc4 $16 { when the open queenside and the weak d6-pawn prevent Black from advancing on the kingside. } ) 
16. b4 Nf6 17. c5 Rf7 18. Nc4 ( { Less precise is } 18. Rc1 { since it is not clear yet where the rook will be most helpful: } Bf8 19. Kh1 Rg7 20. Nc4 h5 { And now the game Brkic – Danielsen, Heraklio 2007, went } 21. cxd6 ( { if instead White played } 21. c6 { now, } b6 { would require White to play a2-a4-a5 and move his rook back to a1 } ) 
cxd6 22. Nb5 a6 23. Nba3 g4 { Black has perfectly good play. } ) 
Bf8 19. a4 Rg7 { Black is following the schematic piece arrangement previously discussed, but the lack of his bishop on c8 makes it harder to execute ...g5-g4. } 20. c6 { If White can establish a solid pawn on c6, he will be positionally winning on the queenside. Here Black is in trouble as he is losing the queenside battle before he has created any kingside threats. } b6 $2 { Worsening Black’s problems by allowing White to open the queenside. } ( { The anti-positional-looking } 20... bxc6 21. dxc6 { was necessary, as it prevents White’s rook from coming into the game. Then } Qc8 $5 22. Nd5 Nxd5 23. Qxd5 Kh8 24. Qd1 Qe6 25. Rc1 $14 { gives a substantial positional advantage for White, but Black can still hope to become active on the kingside with } h6-h5 { and } g5-g4 { . } ) 
21. a5 $1 { After this move White is essentially winning. The c6-pawn combined with an invasion down the a-file will create decisive threats. } Rb8 ( { The alternative } 21... h5 22. Nb5 g4 { loses to } 23. Nxc7 $1 Rxc7 24. axb6 axb6 25. Bxb6 ) 
22. axb6 axb6 23. Nb5 Qc8 { Supporting ...g5-g4, and hoping to transfer the queen to h3 after ...g5-g4xf3. Black would not need to waste time with this if he still had his bishop on c8. } ( 23... h5 { is no better: } 24. Ra7 g4 25. Kh1 { Black’s attack gets nowhere after: } g3 26. hxg3 fxg3 27. Bxg3 h4 28. Bh2 Nh5 29. Ne3 Ng3 30. Kg1 Nf4 31. Ng4 $18 { When nothing can remove this blocking knight, and once again Black’s need for a light-squared bishop is evident. } ) 
24. Ra7 g4 25. Kh1 Ra8 { Making things a little easier for White. } ( { Black’s threats are easily blocked after: } 25... g3 26. hxg3 ( { PH: } 25... Ra8 25. Bxb6 $1 { wins immediately, as after } cxb6 27. Ncxd6 { the passed pawns are too strong. } ) 
fxg3 27. Bxg3 Nh5 28. Bh2 Qd8 ( { or } 28... Ngf4 29. g4 $18 ) 
29. Ne3 Qh4 30. Nf5 $18 ) 
 ( { If } 25... h5 { then } 26. Bxb6 $1 { breaks through Black’s defence. } cxb6 ( { or } 26... Ne8 27. Bxc7 $1 Rxb5 28. Nb6 $18 ) 
27. Ncxd6 Bxd6 28. Nxd6 Qd8 29. Rxg7 Kxg7 30. Nf5 Kf8 31. d6 { And the pawns decide the game. } ) 
26. Rxc7 $1 { +- Black’s position is bad enough to resign already, as his attack is getting nowhere while White will have two extra passed pawns. } Rxc7 27. Nxb6 Qb8 28. Nxa8 Rg7 29. Nb6 g3 30. Bg1 gxh2 31. Bf2 Nh5 32. c7 Rxc7 33. Nxc7 Qxc7 34. Qc1 Qa7 35. Qc2 Ng3+ 36. Kxh2 Qe7 { Threatening Qh4-h1#. } 37. Qc8 ( { White could still spoil the game with } 37. Bxg3 $4 fxg3 38. Kg1 ( 38. Kxg3 Qh4 ) 
Qh4 39. Kf1 Nf4 { with forced mate. But after the text move, Black resigned. Final remarks 1. Black’s need for a light-squared bishop was particularly evident on moves 17-23, as the desired ...g5-g4 break could not be carried out without extra help. 2. White’s c6-pawn was the basis for his victory. Once the a-file became open, Black became defenceless against the potential piece sacrifices to turn the c6-pawn into a winning passed pawn. } ) 

