[White "16) KID Type III"]
[Black "Loek van Wely vs. Sergey Krylov"]
[Result "1-0"]

 { Loek van Wely – Sergey Krylov, Mallorca 2004 Learning objective: This game is an example of how White may block the kingside with g2-g4, and then have a free hand to advance with his queenside plans. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. b4 Nh5 10. Re1 Kh8 { This move has fallen out of fashion in the last three years, possibly due to Black’s poor scores. } ( { Much more common is } 10... f5 11. Ng5 Nf6 12. Bf3 c6 13. Be3 h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 { with a complex and highly analyzed position. } ) 
11. Nd2 Nf4 12. Bf1 f5 13. g3 ( { Another option is } 13. a4 $5 a5 14. bxa5 Rxa5 15. Nb3 Ra8 16. a5 fxe4 17. Nxe4 { as in Sokolov – Arakhamia-Grant, Gibraltar 2009. } ) 
Nh5 14. c5 f4 $2 { A general piece of advice for King’s Indian players is to decide beforehand whether an opposite-flanks race is likely to work or not. If Black is likely to lose the race, then he should not start it at all, and instead should attempt to find counter-chances by keeping the central tension. In this case, the way White’s kingside structure is set up should be a red flag for Black. In previous examples Black’s attack has proven effective due to the advance ...g6-g5-g4-g3, but what is Black supposed to do in this case? Any feasible attack will take much longer, while White’s forces are already arriving at the queenside. } ( { Much more principled is } 14... Nf6 { (preparing ...fxe4) and now } 15. cxd6 $5 ( { while } 14... f4 14. Bg2 { can be answered by the unorthodox } dxc5 $5 16. bxc5 fxe4 17. Ndxe4 Nfxd5 { with level chances. } ) 
cxd6 16. b5 fxe4 17. Ndxe4 { yields a level position } ) 
15. Nc4 Ng8 ( { Not: } 15... g5 $4 16. Qxh5 $18 ) 
 ( { A reasonable try was } 15... Nf6 16. a4 g5 { though after } 17. Ra2 Ng6 18. Rc2 g4 19. Ba3 $16 { it seems Black will be unable to pose a real threat to White’s kingside. } ) 
16. a4 { This is a fine multi-purpose move: it expands on the queenside and clears a2 for the rook to defend the second rank. } Ngf6 17. Ra2 Ne8 ( { Black’s last two moves prepared } 17... g5 $2 { but unfortunately White is winning after } 18. cxd6 cxd6 19. Nb5 { since the d6-pawn cannot be protected. } ) 
18. Rc2 ( { Another option was } 18. g4 $5 Nhf6 19. f3 { , which is similar to the game. } ) 
Bd7 19. g4 $1 { ± A good decision; Black’s attack was not really threatening, but it is better to simply close the kingside immediately, in order to have a free hand on the queenside. } Nhf6 20. f3 h5 $2 ( { A healthier alternative was } 20... g5 21. b5 h5 { but after } 22. h3 $16 { Black does not seem to be getting anywhere, while White’s queenside expansion keeps on rolling. } ) 
21. g5 $1 { Closing the kingside decisively. } Nh7 22. h4 $18 { Black’s only hope to create counterplay on the kingside is to sacrifice a piece with ...Nxg5, but this is very unlikely to work, as White may easily blockade the kingside pawns, or simply continue pursuing his already-advanced queenside plan. White, on the other hand, has a very significant spatial advantage over the entire board, and the remainder of this game is an illustration of how he may exploit his enormous positional advantage. } Rf7 23. Na5 Bc8 ( 23... b6 { , attempting to open up some lines, doesn’t help due to } 24. cxb6 cxb6 25. Nc6 Qc8 26. b5 $5 { +- followed by Ra2 and a4-a5. } ) 
24. c6 $1 { Gaining even more space. As before, the c6-pawn secures White a winning queenside advantage. Since in this game Black cannot find counterplay on the kingside, the rest of the game is rather easily decided. } b6 { Accepting a lost position. } ( { But } 24... bxc6 $2 { loses after } 25. Nxc6 Qd7 26. Rh2 $1 { followed by Bh3 trapping the queen. } ) 
25. Nc4 { The threat is a4-a5 opening up the a-file immediately, thus it is necessary to play: } a6 { A new phase in the game has begun. Black’s pieces are so constrained they cannot play, hence Black will spend the next twenty moves simply waiting for the end. Meanwhile, White has complete freedom to manoeuvre in order to find the right winning method. Note how Black is ready to close the queenside. If White plays a4-a5, then Black replies ...b6-b5, and if White plays b4-b5, then Black replies ...a6-a5. Therefore, when White decides to push one of his queenside pawns, he will need to think how exactly he desires to sacrifice a piece in order to break Black’s last line of defence. } 26. Rh2 { This prophylactic move is intended to keep an eye on the kingside in case Black attempts the shake things up with ...Nxg5. } ( { If } 26. a5 b5 { the position is momentarily closed. } ) 
Kg8 27. Ree2 { The manoeuvring begins. } Nf8 28. Bd2 Nh7 29. Be1 Nf8 30. Ra2 Nh7 31. Kh1 Nf8 32. Bf2 { Note how all of White’s pieces are aiming toward the queenside. } Rb8 33. Bd3 Nh7 34. b5 { The positional threat is a4-a5 opening up two decisive lines on the queenside, and there is nothing better than: } a5 { The game is entirely closed, and now White will prepare the decisive break. } ( { Even worse is } 34... axb5 $2 35. axb5 { as White wins on the spot after } Bf8 36. Qa4 Bg7 37. Qa7 { . } ) 
 ( { If } 34... Ra8 { then } 35. a5 $1 { opens up the queenside decisively: } axb5 36. Nxb5 bxa5 37. Be1 Bf8 38. Nxa5 $18 { The c6-pawn prevents Black’s manoeuvres, making White’s queenside invasion easy. } ) 
35. Ne2 { The beginning of a lengthy yet winning manoeuvre: Nc3-e2-c1-b3xa5. } Bf8 ( { Attempting to create counterplay with } 35... Nxg5 $5 { is refuted by: } 36. hxg5 Qxg5 37. Qg1 $1 Qh6 { Black hopes to push ...g6-g5 , but now } 38. Bxb6 $1 cxb6 39. Nxb6 { decides the game due to the creation of two powerful passed pawns. } ) 
36. Nc1 Bg7 ( { Again } 36... Nxg5 { is met by } 37. hxg5 Qxg5 38. Qg1 $5 { +- } ( { or } 37... Bg7 37. Bh4 Qh6 39. Qg1 $18 ) 
 ) 
37. Nb3 Bf8 38. Bf1 { There is no hurry. } Be7 39. Qe1 Ra8 ( { Now } 39... Nxg5 $5 { can be met by } 40. hxg5 Bxg5 41. Bg1 $5 { followed by Rag2 and wins. } ) 
40. Ra3 Bf8 41. Nbxa5 $1 { White gives up a piece to open up the game; the a- and b-pawns are unstoppable. } bxa5 42. Nxa5 Ng7 43. Nc4 Nxg5 { A desperate attempt for counterplay. } ( { Of course waiting will not help: } 43... Rb8 44. Rb3 Ne8 45. a5 Ng7 46. b6 Ba6 47. b7 { Followed by Nd2 and pushing the a-pawn. } ) 
44. hxg5 Qxg5 45. a5 Qd8 46. b6 cxb6 47. axb6 { White has two passed pawns on the sixth rank; the rest is simple. } Rxa3 48. Nxa3 g5 49. Qb4 g4 50. Bh4 Be7 51. b7 Bxh4 52. b8=Q { Final remarks 1. Black’s biggest mistake was attempting a kingside attack with 14...f4. Instead, Black should have transformed the structure into a King’s Indian Type III-B with 14...Nf6 and 15...fxe4 obtaining a playable position by analogy with the previous games. 2. Unlike previous examples, White’s kingside pawns on f2-g3-h2 are hard to attack, as there is no clear target. This fact made Black’s attack ineffective, besides which Black was already far behind when this race began. 3. The reason White was winning after 22. h4 was that he had an enormous space advantage all over the board. As we will see in the next example, closing the kingside by itself does not guarantee an advantage if Black can secure some space on the queenside. Throughout this book I have been hesitant to include rapid games (et alone blindfold chess), as I prefer games played at the highest level. In this case, I thought it was worthwhile making an exception, as in the next game Carlsen’s execution is simply superb and an ideal example for the position. } 
 1-0

